# Java Azure Functions Development Standards

## Code Style and Structure

### Naming Conventions
- **Class Names**: Use PascalCase (e.g., `UserService`, `OrderRepository`)
- **Method and Variable Names**: Use camelCase (e.g., `findUserById`, `isOrderValid`)
- **Constants**: Use ALL_CAPS (e.g., `MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`, `DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE`)
- **Package Names**: Use lowercase (e.g., `org.cloud.demo.config`)

### Package Structure
```
org.cloud.demo/
├── config/          # Configuration classes
├── model/           # Data models
├── service/         # Business logic services
├── repository/      # Data access layer
├── util/            # Utility classes
└── Function.java    # Azure Functions main class
```

### Class Responsibilities
- Each class should have a single responsibility
- Service classes handle business logic
- Configuration classes manage configuration
- Utility classes provide common functionality
- Model classes represent data structures

## Azure Functions Best Practices

### Function Design
- Each function should focus on a single task
- Use appropriate trigger types (HTTP, Blob, Timer, etc.)
- Implement proper error handling and logging
- Use environment variables for configuration

### Configuration Management
```java
// Use configuration class to manage environment variables
public class ApplicationConfig {
    public String getEnvironment() {
        return System.getenv("APP_ENVIRONMENT");
    }
    
    public boolean isDebugMode() {
        String debugMode = System.getenv("DEBUG_MODE");
        return "true".equalsIgnoreCase(debugMode);
    }
}
```

### Error Handling
```java
// Unified error handling
try {
    // Business logic
} catch (Exception e) {
    context.getLogger().severe("Error: " + e.getMessage());
    return ResponseUtil.createErrorResponse(request, HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, 
        "Operation failed: " + e.getMessage());
}
```

### Logging
```java
// Structured logging
context.getLogger().info("Processing file: " + fileName + " (" + fileSize + " bytes)");
context.getLogger().warning("Invalid data format at line " + lineNumber);
context.getLogger().severe("Database connection failed: " + error.getMessage());
```

## Code Quality

### Comments and Documentation
- Add JavaDoc comments for all public methods
- Use English comments to explain complex business logic
- Add descriptive comments for classes

