### Exception Handling
- Catch specific exception types
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log detailed error information
- Avoid swallowing exceptions

### Resource Management
- Use try-with-resources statements
- Ensure database connections and file handles are properly closed
- Implement appropriate cleanup logic

## Security

### Sensitive Information Handling
- Use Azure Key Vault to store keys and passwords
- Mask sensitive information in logs
- Use environment variables instead of hardcoded values

```java
/**
 * Mask sensitive information
 */
private String maskSecret(String secret) {
    if (secret == null || secret.isEmpty()) {
        return "not set";
    }
    if (secret.length() <= 4) {
        return "****";
    }
    return secret.substring(0, 2) + "****" + secret.substring(secret.length() - 2);
}
```

### Input Validation
- Validate all user inputs
- Check file formats and sizes
- Prevent SQL injection and XSS attacks

## Performance Optimization

### Database Operations
- Use connection pools
- Implement appropriate indexes
- Avoid N+1 query problems
- Use batch operations

### Memory Management
- Avoid memory leaks
- Use appropriate data structures
- Release unused resources promptly

## Testing

### Unit Testing
- Write tests for all business logic
- Use mock objects to isolate dependencies
- Test boundary conditions and error cases

### Integration Testing
- Test Azure Functions endpoints
- Verify database operations
- Test external service integrations

## Deployment and Monitoring

### Configuration Management
- Use environment variables to distinguish development, testing, and production environments
- Implement configuration validation
- Use Azure App Configuration for configuration management

### Monitoring and Logging
- Implement structured logging
- Use Azure Application Insights for monitoring
- Set up appropriate alerts and metrics

## Code Review Checklist

### Functional Correctness
- [ ] Code implements expected functionality
- [ ] All boundary cases are handled
- [ ] Error handling is appropriate

### Code Quality
- [ ] Follows naming conventions
- [ ] Code structure is clear
- [ ] No duplicate code
- [ ] Appropriate comments and documentation

### Security
- [ ] No hardcoded sensitive information
- [ ] Appropriate input validation implemented
- [ ] Error messages don't leak sensitive information

### Performance
- [ ] No obvious performance issues
- [ ] Resource usage is appropriate
- [ ] Database queries are optimized

### Maintainability
- [ ] Code is easy to understand and modify
- [ ] Appropriate abstraction and separation of concerns
- [ ] Sufficient test coverage

## Common Anti-patterns

### Practices to Avoid
- Mixing multiple responsibilities in functions
- Hardcoding configuration values
- Ignoring exceptions or errors
- Using global variables
- Overly complex nested structures
- Lack of appropriate logging

### Recommended Practices
- Single responsibility principle
- Dependency injection
- Appropriate error handling
- Structured logging
- Externalized configuration
- Unit test coverage
